<%- include('../partials/header', { title: 'The Chronicle' }) %>
<%
  const german = A.german;
  const flag = name => [...german].some(g => String(name).includes(g)) ? '🇩🇪' : '🇺🇸';
  const dollars = n => (n == null ? '' : '$' + Number(n).toLocaleString('en-US'));
  const champByYear = {};
  for (const c of A.champions) champByYear[c.year] = c;
  const years = [];
  for (let y = 2026; y >= (A.lore.foundingYear || 2016); y--) years.push(y);
  // Season pages exist only where box scores do (2023-2025). Pre-Sleeper years
  // are not yet clickable (their ceremonial chapter is pending).
  const linkFor = y => A.byYear[y] ? `/history/season/${y}` : null;
%>

<div class="chron">
  <!-- 1. HEADER (turnover-corrected eyebrow) -->
  <p class="chron-eyebrow">Est. 2016 · Not Quite the Same Ten Men · A Decade of Receipts</p>
  <h1 class="chron-title">The <span class="gold">MFGA</span> Archive</h1>
  <p class="chron-sub">Every dollar, every champion, every humiliation — written down at last.</p>

  <!-- 2. THE STORY OF THE LEAGUE (the centerpiece) -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="body">
      <div class="chapter">
        <p>It began, as the great institutions do, with a dick joke. In
        <strong>2016</strong> ten men put down a hundred dollars apiece and founded
        the <strong>Balls and Wieners League</strong> — a name chosen by grown adults
        with jobs, and the first of three, because this league has never once been
        able to leave well enough alone. Balls and Wieners became the <em>Whiny
        Little Bitch League</em>, which lasted years and told you everything you
        needed to know about the customer-service tone of the group chat, and in
        <strong>2024</strong> it finally became <strong>Make Football Great Again</strong>.
        Three names in a decade. Not one of them a name you'd say in front of your
        mother.</p>

        <p>The buy-in climbed like a fever: <span class="stat">$100</span> to
        <span class="stat">$125</span> to <span class="stat">$150</span> and on up,
        a hundred a rung, to <span class="stat">$400</span> today — with a vote on
        the floor right now to make it five. And at every single increase
        <strong>David</strong>, the German, has objected, the way other men object
        to sunrise: annually, loudly, and to no effect. He was the league's first
        champion in 2016 and he has been bitching about the cost of playing in it
        ever since. Somewhere along the way the league bolted on <strong>keepers</strong>
        (2024, the first draft anyone actually protected a player), and — the quiet
        revolution — in <strong>2023</strong> it added the <strong>weekly-high $100</strong>,
        a hundred bucks to the top score each week of the regular season. Nobody
        clocked it at the time. It is now <span class="stat">37.5%</span> of the
        entire pot. The biggest money in the league is the prize nobody voted to
        make the biggest money in the league.</p>

        <p>The chairs have not all held the same asses, either — whatever the old
        tagline claimed. <strong>Three men left and three men took their seats</strong>:
        <strong>Sam</strong>, <strong>Dylan</strong> and <strong>Jeremy</strong>
        replaced <strong>Brandon</strong>, <strong>Taylor Hagen</strong> and
        <strong>Ben</strong>. The league does not keep good records of its own
        casualties — Brandon and Taylor Hagen have been scrubbed from the ledger
        entirely, and Ben survives only as a name in one draft order, a ghost at
        the table. The chronicle names them anyway, because a league that erases
        the men it replaced is just lying to itself with extra steps.</p>

        <p>What the money remembers is the dynasties. <strong>Marian</strong> owns
        the early book — a genuine three-peat, <span class="stat">2019</span>,
        <span class="stat">2020</span>, <span class="stat">2021</span>, and then a
        fourth ring in <span class="stat">2022</span> that the league has refused
        to fully honor for reasons we will get to. Then the crown moved to
        <strong>Michael</strong>, who took <span class="stat">2023</span> and
        <span class="stat">2025</span> and turned Dixie Wrecked into the closest
        thing this league has to a modern dynasty. In between,
        <strong>Jeremy</strong> — the doctor, and yes, the league mocks him for
        being a doctor — diagnosed the whole field in 2024 and walked off with the
        ring while everyone else bled out.</p>

        <p>And then there's everyone else, which is the good part. <strong>Dylan</strong>
        named his team after himself and still can't get over the hump. <strong>Bates</strong>
        has spent years branded the league's Chiefs homer, a reputation that
        collapses the instant you check the receipts — <span class="stat">three</span>
        Kansas City picks in three seasons, fewer than the two Germans, so the most
        notorious KC fan in the league got out-Chiefed by men who've never claimed
        to give a shit about Missouri. <strong>Richard</strong> is the league's
        patron saint of the bad beat: in 2025 he scored <span class="stat">1,711</span>
        points, third-most in the whole league, and finished <em>dead last</em> at
        <span class="stat">4–11</span>. <strong>Sam</strong> owns a co-championship
        and, more recently, the wooden spoon — the man contains multitudes.
        <strong>Justin</strong> keeps quietly cashing checks nobody sees him earn.
        And <strong>Cory</strong>, the commissioner, runs a team called <em>Zero
        Mistakes Were Made</em> and in 2024 missed the playoffs by
        <span class="stat">8.20</span> points while leaving <span class="stat">297</span>
        on his own bench — the man built the scoreboard and then benched his own
        money on it. He gets ripped hardest in here, on purpose. It's his page.</p>

        <p>The one story told in a lower voice is the <strong>2022 asterisk</strong>
        — Marian's fourth title, shared with Sam, disputed to this day. That one
        gets stated straight, once, at the bottom of this page, because the joke is
        the ring and the decade of arguing about it, never the reason it happened.</p>

        <p>A decade in, the Balls and Wieners League has a buy-in of four hundred
        dollars, ten owners who aren't quite the ten it started with, a
        controversial banner year nobody agrees on, and — finally — a website that
        remembers <em>every single dollar</em> any of them has ever won or lost.
        That's what this archive is. The full constitution — the amendment ledger,
        the buy-in ladder, the payout revisions and the pending votes — lives in
        <a href="/history/amendments" style="color:var(--gold-bright)">the Amendments</a>.
        The rest is the seasons. Read them and weep, or read them and gloat. Those
        are the only two options this league has ever offered.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3. THE SEASONS timeline -->
  <div class="card">
    <h2>📖 The Seasons <span class="sub">tap a year with a chapter</span></h2>
    <div class="body">
      <div class="timeline">
        <% for (const y of years) {
             const c = champByYear[y];
             const href = linkFor(y);
             const written = chapters.has(y);
             const preSleeper = y < 2023;
             const inProgress = y === 2026;
        %>
          <% if (href) { %><a class="tl-row" href="<%= href %>"><% } else { %><div class="tl-row tl-dead"><% } %>
            <span class="tl-year"><%= y %></span>
            <span class="tl-mid">
              <% if (inProgress) { %>
                <span class="tl-champ tl-live">Season in progress</span>
              <% } else if (c) { %>
                <span class="tl-champ">🏆 <%= c.champion %> <span class="flag"><%= flag(c.champion) %></span><% if (c.asterisk) { %><a class="aster" href="#asterisk" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">*</a><% } %></span>
                <% if (c.payout != null) { %><span class="tl-won"><%= dollars(c.payout) %></span><% } %>
              <% } %>
            </span>
            <span class="tl-tag">
              <% if (written) { %><span class="tag">Chapter</span>
              <% } else if (A.byYear[y]) { %><span class="tag pending">Records</span>
              <% } else if (preSleeper) { %><span class="tag ghost">Pre-Sleeper</span>
              <% } %>
            </span>
          <% if (href) { %></a><% } else { %></div><% } %>
        <% } %>
      </div>
      <p class="tl-note">Full box scores survive from <b>2023</b> on. 2016–2022 are the age before Sleeper — champions and money only; their chapters are on the way.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. FOUR DOORS -->
  <div class="doors">
    <a class="door" href="/history/records">
      <span class="door-icon">📖</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Record Book</span>
      <span class="door-sub">All-time highs, lows, the dynasty tracker, and every franchise page.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/money">
      <span class="door-icon">💰</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Money Board</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Career earnings, ranked ten deep. The table that settles arguments.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/badbeats">
      <span class="door-icon">💔</span>
      <span class="door-title">Bad Beats Hall of Fame</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Auto-detected heartbreak — the tragedies the schedule authored.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/catalogue">
      <span class="door-icon">🃏</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Absurdity Catalogue</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Every box score mined: kickers over QBs, benched studs, near-misses.</span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. THE 2022 ASTERISK (closing footnote) -->
  <%- include('_asterisk') %>

  <p class="provenance">
    Derived from the master sheet (<%= A.provenance.master_sha256.slice(0, 12) %>…)
    and the Sleeper harvest of <%= (A.provenance.harvest_built_at || '').slice(0, 10) %>.
  </p>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
